<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/water-ceiling.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/water-ceiling-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/water-ceiling.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="795" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/claim-appraisals/">Appraisals</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Water Damage Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Water Damage Claim Appraisal for New York Homes and Buildings</h1><p class="lede">Water damage claim appraisal helps when the insurer pays for a few feet of drywall but the water travelled much further. We trace the full path of the leak and value the drying, demolition and rebuild it really takes.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-orange" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Sources</dt><dd>Pipes, appliances, unit leaks</dd></div><div><dt>Often missed</dt><dd>Hidden migration, matching</dd></div><div><dt>Check</dt><dd>Sewer backup endorsement</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Water travels, estimates often do not</h2>
<p>Water is the most common claim we see in New York, and the most commonly underscoped. A frozen pipe in a Rochester exterior wall, a failed washing machine hose in a Queens attached home or a leak that runs three floors down in a Manhattan co-op will all spread behind finishes. A proper <strong>water damage claim appraisal</strong> follows that path with moisture readings and photos, not guesswork.</p>
<h2>Losses we value</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Burst and frozen pipes</strong>, especially after cold snaps in Buffalo, Syracuse and the North Country</li>
<li><strong>Appliance leaks</strong> from dishwashers, refrigerators, water heaters and washers</li>
<li><strong>Unit-to-unit leaks</strong> in co-ops and condos, where several policies may respond</li>
<li><strong>Sewer and drain backup</strong>, where you carry the optional endorsement</li>
<li><strong>Mold remediation</strong> to the extent your policy covers it, which is often limited</li>
</ul>
<h2>The policy wording behind water claims</h2>
<p>Most homeowners policies cover sudden and accidental discharge but exclude long-term seepage, flood and, unless endorsed, sewer backup. Many also require you to keep heat on or drain the plumbing in a vacant home. Those are coverage questions. Once the insurer accepts the loss, appraisal is about how much drying, removal and rebuild is reasonable.</p>
<p>Within an accepted claim, the disputes are usually practical. How much drywall must come out to dry a wall cavity properly? Can hardwood that cupped across a whole floor be refinished, or does it need replacing to stay continuous through adjoining rooms? Should cabinets with swollen particleboard bases be replaced as a set? Was the mitigation invoice reasonable for the equipment and days used? Insurers sometimes trim each of these items modestly, and the total gap becomes large. We answer them with moisture data, product information and photos rather than opinion, and we price the rebuild for the finishes you actually had, not builder-grade defaults.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Sewer backup is an add-on</b>Backup through floor drains and toilets is usually covered only by an optional endorsement, often with a low limit. Groundwater and surface flooding, as many Queens basements saw during the remnants of Ida in 2021, are generally flood. See our <a href="/policyholder-guides/basement-water-sewer-backup-vs-flood/">basement water guide</a>.</div>
<h2>Shared walls, shared claims</h2>
<p>In co-ops and condos, the master policy, the unit owner’s HO-6 and sometimes the upstairs neighbour’s policy all touch one leak. We help sort which items belong to which policy before anything is appraised; the <a href="/claim-appraisals/co-op-condo-commercial-appraisal/">co-op, condo and commercial appraisal</a> page explains more, and the <a href="/appraisal-calculators/co-op-condo-claim-splitter/">claim splitter</a> helps sort line items.</p>
<h2>What to keep for the panel</h2>
<p>Mitigation company drying logs and moisture maps, plumber invoices naming the failed component, photos before demolition, and receipts for damaged contents. These records carry real weight with an umpire because they were created at the time of loss.</p>

<h2>Questions about water damage appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is a frozen pipe covered in New York?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies cover sudden damage from a frozen pipe if you took reasonable care to maintain heat or drain the system. Vacancy and maintenance conditions vary, so check your policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays when water from the unit above damages my apartment?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the building documents and the policies involved. Often the master policy covers building elements and your HO-6 covers your improvements and contents.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can mold costs be appraised?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If your policy covers mold remediation and the dispute is about the cost, it can usually be included. Many policies cap mold coverage at a low limit.</p></div></details></div>
